{"book":null,"builtIso":"2026-10-03T06:31:51Z","edges":{"in":[{"basis":"60d","breaks":"Crypto-native shocks — an exchange failure, a forced liquidation cascade — swamp the liquidity signal for weeks at a time.","conducts":0.55,"from":"netliq","identity":false,"lag":"weeks","lead":0,"mechanism":"Bitcoin has no earnings to fall back on, so its price is close to a pure function of how much spare money exists.","n":60,"r250":0.07458942560848703,"r60":0.014487661611038793,"sign":1,"to":"btc","verdict":"quiet","weight":0.5,"why":""},{"basis":"60d","breaks":"","conducts":1.0,"from":"real10y","identity":false,"lag":"weeks","lead":0,"mechanism":"The same opportunity-cost argument as gold, applied to another asset with no yield — but far noisier, because bitcoin's own cycle usually dominates.","n":60,"r250":-0.11659146046511418,"r60":-0.3230758740540491,"sign":-1,"to":"btc","verdict":"agrees","weight":0.25,"why":""},{"basis":"60d","breaks":"Regime-dependent and frequently absent. Bitcoin has rallied through rising-yield stretches more than once, and when it does the liquidity and adoption stories are simply larger than the discount-rate one.","conducts":1.0,"from":"us10y","identity":false,"lag":"weeks","lead":0,"mechanism":"Bitcoin has no cash flow to discount, so it competes head-on with the risk-free rate: the more a government bond pays for doing nothing, the higher the bar for holding something that pays nothing at all.","n":60,"r250":-0.056285485409543694,"r60":-0.2944750512255515,"sign":-1,"to":"btc","verdict":"agrees","weight":0.25,"why":""},{"basis":"60d","breaks":"Vanishes whenever bitcoin is trading on something of its own — an ETF flow, a halving, a failure.","conducts":1.0,"from":"dxy","identity":false,"lag":"days","lead":0,"mechanism":"Bitcoin is priced in dollars and bought largely with borrowed ones. A stronger dollar is both a lower quoted number and a tighter world.","n":60,"r250":-0.1538629921288948,"r60":-0.3228028571134264,"sign":-1,"to":"btc","verdict":"a